{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target dismisses the idempotent/safe distinction as a 'category error' by redefining 'safe' to mean 'no side effects' (a purity notion). In the published distributed-systems definitions the problem points to, 'safe' does NOT mean side-effect-free; a safe operation may have side effects as long as repeated execution after the first leaves the final observable state unchanged (e.g., POST that creates a record once and no-ops on retry), while 'idempotent' means repeated execution produces the same result/effect each time (e.g., PUT/DELETE). By collapsing 'safe' into 'has no side effects' and declaring it a contradiction, the target fails to state the precise distinction the problem demands and mischaracterizes the safety property, making its conclusion that 'safety is irrelevant' unsupported.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}